# Default OpenAI API Configuration
# Copy this file to .env and set your actual values

# LLM Provider Configuration
# Supported providers: openai, anthropic, openrouter, ollama
LLM_PROVIDER=openai

# OpenAI Configuration (when LLM_PROVIDER=openai)
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_MODEL_NAME=gpt-5-2025-08-07

# Anthropic Configuration (when LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_MODEL_NAME=claude-sonnet-4-5

# OpenRouter Configuration (when LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter)
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_MODEL_NAME=google/gemini-2.5-pro
OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# Ollama Configuration (when LLM_PROVIDER=ollama)
# Vision-capable models: gemma3:4b, llama3.2-vision, llava
# NOTE: qwen2.5vl may struggle with circulation vs libration distinction
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
# OLLAMA_MODEL_NAME=qwen2.5vl:7b
OLLAMA_MODEL_NAME=gemma3:4b

# Ollama-specific prompt template is defined in config.py with a sensible default
# Uncomment and customize if needed (must be a single line with \n for newlines):
# OLLAMA_PROMPT_TEMPLATE="Your custom prompt here..."

# ===========================================
# HuggingFace Configuration (cross-platform)
# ===========================================
# Use 'huggingface' provider for local GPU inference
# LLM_PROVIDER=huggingface

# Model from HuggingFace Hub
HF_MODEL_NAME=Qwen/Qwen2-VL-7B-Instruct

# Quantization: none, 4bit, or 8bit
# 4bit/8bit require bitsandbytes: pip install bitsandbytes
HF_QUANTIZATION=none

# Device mapping strategy: auto, cuda:0, cpu
HF_DEVICE_MAP=auto

# Torch dtype: float16, bfloat16, float32
HF_TORCH_DTYPE=bfloat16

# Max new tokens for generation
HF_MAX_NEW_TOKENS=256

# 4-bit quantization options (when HF_QUANTIZATION=4bit)
HF_4BIT_QUANT_TYPE=nf4
HF_4BIT_USE_DOUBLE_QUANT=true
HF_4BIT_COMPUTE_DTYPE=bfloat16

# ===========================================
# MLX Configuration (macOS Apple Silicon only)
# ===========================================
# Use 'mlx' provider for native Apple Silicon inference
# LLM_PROVIDER=mlx

# Model from mlx-community (pre-quantized)
# Available models: https://huggingface.co/mlx-community
# MLX_MODEL_NAME=mlx-community/Qwen2-VL-2B-Instruct-4bit
MLX_MODEL_NAME=mlx-community/gemma-3n-E2B-it-4bit

# Generation parameters
MLX_MAX_TOKENS=256
MLX_TEMPERATURE=0.0
MLX_VERBOSE=false

PROMPT_TEMPLATE_SIMPLIFIED="You are classifying resonant-angle plots into exactly two labels: \"resonant\" or \"non-resonant\" (transient behavior counts as resonant).\n\nFollow this deterministic checklist:\n1. BOUNDED PATCH TEST \u2192 Scan the whole x-axis for any slice (at least ~8\u201310% of the width) where the points stay inside one or two horizontal bands, islands, closed loops, or turning-point \"beads\" with obvious blank space immediately above AND below that slice. The neighboring rows should look empty (pure black background) in that y-interval for the duration of the slice; merely having slightly lower density is not enough. If other diagonals, noise, or wrap-around streaks keep appearing inside that band, the test is NO. Accept slow drifts, tilted pillows, thick sausages, paired bands, or flattened caps only when they linger long enough to leave those empty buffers. Ignore tiny caps or noisy clumps that appear for just a couple of pixels before the track resumes full-height motion.\n2. FULL-HEIGHT SWEEP TEST \u2192 Mark this TRUE whenever the trajectory spends essentially the entire width in straight diagonal wrap-around stripes or chaotic peppering that repeatedly touches both y=0 and y=2\u03c0 with no clean horizontal respite. If those diagonals keep marching through the same y-interval where you hoped for an island, the sweep test wins and bounded_patch must be NO. Only set this test to FALSE when you clearly see an extended pause, plateau, or closed island surrounded by empty gaps.\n3. CLASSIFY \u2192 If bounded_patch=yes anywhere, the answer must be resonant (transient or pure). Only respond non-resonant when bounded_patch=no and full_sweep=yes.\n\nHelpful cues:\n- Slow/secular librations look like tilted pillows or wide noise bands that never unwrap all the way; these are resonant.\n- Transient plots often mix diagonal circulation with a resonant island on one edge; the presence of that island (with clear empty margins) forces a resonant label.\n- True circulation shows clean zebra-like diagonals or full-height chaotic spray with zero horizontal dwell time; there are no islands with exclusive empty bands above and below.\n- Horizontal caps at the top/bottom count only when they occupy a noticeable width (\u224810% of the axis) AND remain isolated from the rest of the trace; fleeting crossings or caps that immediately bleed into full-height motion do NOT qualify.\n\nExample mappings:\n- Left half diagonal, right half horizontal island \u2192 resonant.\n- Entire plot is diagonal stripes that wrap top\u2194bottom with no gaps or pauses \u2192 non-resonant.\n- Thick noisy arc that wanders but never reaches both extremes \u2192 resonant.\n\nRespond with lowercase JSON on one line. Set the subtype to capture the checklist in the form 'bounded_patch=<yes/no>; full_sweep=<yes/no>; brief evidence' so the decision is auditable (e.g., {\"status\": \"resonant\", \"subtype\": \"bounded_patch=yes; full_sweep=no; island on right\"})."
PROMPT_TEMPLATE="You are an expert astronomer specializing in the analysis of orbital dynamics from scatter plots. Your task is to classify a given plot of a resonant angle versus time into one of three categories: resonant, non-resonant, transient.\n\nresonant: the data points are confined within clear horizontal boundaries for the entire duration of the plot. The plot can be within these boundaries OR it can be split into two blocks connected to the top and bottom borders, where each block stay within its own boundaries. In this case, one can draw a rectangle in the vertically middle of the plot that has NO dots in it. If there are no such boundaries, the plot is NOT resonant.\n\nnon-resonant: chaotic scatter that fills most or all of the vertical space OR clear diagonal lines that \"wrap around\" from the top of the plot to the bottom. The lines can be noisy or have some oscillations but the general trend is parallel diagonal lines. If this behavior lasts ALL the plot, then it is non-resonant.\n\ntransient: if the plot does not fall in resonant or non-resonant, then it's transient. Transient means that there is a piece of the plot that behaves as resonant but not all the time.\n\nNote: some plots can be noisy and have several \"trends\" or mixed behaviors. In this case, it's better to classify them as transient.\n\nTo perform the classification, follow the steps:\n1. Split image into blocks ~10% each.\n2. Classify each block.\n3. If each block is resonant, the status is resonant.\n4. If each block is non-resonant, the status is non-resonant.\n5. In all other cases, it is transient.\n\nClassify the image in one of these categories following the algorithm above."
SIMPLE_PROMPT_TEMPLATE="You are an expert astronomer specializing in the analysis of orbital dynamics from scatter plots. Your task is to classify a given plot of a resonant angle versus time into one of two categories: resonant, non-resonant. Review it as an image. Rules: non-resonant: when there is chaotic scatter that fills ALL of the vertical space OR clear diagonal lines that wrap around from the top of the plot to the bottom. The lines can be noisy or have some small oscillations but the general trend is parallel diagonal lines or random scatter across the ENTIRE plot. There should be no oscillations (even short ones) or two blocks of oscillations near the borders. resonant: if there are some oscillations OR a part of them, the status is ALWAYS resonant. Important: you must review the WHOLE graph, not a part of it."

